## 10.2 Existing text editing

Support genuine modification of existing PDF text wherever technically possible.

Users should be able to:
- Select text objects.
- Modify text.
- Change font size.
- Change supported fonts.
- Change font weight.
- Change colour.
- Adjust alignment.
- Change position.
- Delete text.

Preserve layout, spacing, and formatting as accurately as possible.

Do not present text overlays as genuine native text editing.

When native modification is not possible, clearly communicate the alternative.

## 10.3 Object manipulation

Support:
- Text objects.
- Images.
- Shapes.
- Vector elements where supported.
- Annotations.
- Editor-managed elements.

Allow:
- Select.
- Move.
- Resize.
- Rotate.
- Delete.
- Duplicate.
- Align.
- Group and ungroup where supported.
- Change stacking order.
- Inspect properties.

## 10.4 Object visibility, hiding, and removal

**This functionality is mandatory.**

Provide an object-management experience inspired by object manipulation in Sajda PDF.

Users must be able to select supported document objects and manage their visibility or remove them.

### Hide objects

Allow:
- Hide selected object.
- Show hidden object.
- Hide multiple selected objects.
- Toggle visibility in the object panel.
- Identify hidden objects.
- Restore hidden objects.

Make it clear whether visibility is only an editor working-state change or is reflected in the exported PDF.

Do not confuse reversible hiding with permanent removal.

### Remove objects

Allow:
- Delete selected object.
- Delete multiple objects.
- Remove selected text blocks.
- Remove selected images.
- Remove supported shapes.
- Remove annotations.
- Remove editor-managed elements.
- Delete using context menu.
- Delete using keyboard shortcuts.
- Undo deletion.

The exported document must reflect actual removal.

Do not merely place a white rectangle over an object and call it deleted.

### Object management panel

Provide a dedicated object/layer panel.

Where supported, show:
- Object type.
- Name or descriptive label.
- Visibility.
- Lock status.
- Selection status.
- Stacking order.

Allow:
- Select.
- Rename editor-managed objects.
- Hide.
- Show.
- Lock.
- Unlock.
- Delete.
- Reorder.
- Multi-select.

### Contextual object actions

Expose relevant actions:
- Hide.
- Show.
- Delete.
- Duplicate.
- Lock.
- Unlock.
- Bring forward.
- Send backward.
- Bring to front.
- Send to back.
- Align.
- Resize.
- Rotate.
- Inspect properties.

Only expose actions that genuinely work for the selected object type.

### Technical requirements

PDFs may contain complex content streams, grouped text runs, clipping paths, embedded images, vector paths, transparency groups, Form XObjects, and annotations.

Do not assume that every visible element corresponds to one independently editable object.

Implement object selection and deletion based on the actual document structure and processing capabilities.

Where safe isolation is impossible, communicate the limitation rather than deleting unrelated content.

### Acceptance criteria

For supported objects, users must be able to:
1. Select an object.
2. Hide it.
3. Restore its visibility.
4. Remove it.
5. Undo removal.
6. Export the modified document.
7. Verify that removal is reflected in the resulting PDF.

